{"run_id": "b717dc7169cf4570a0bfd13dceeb0c86", "study": "output_length_natural_pilot", "model_key": "gpt_oss_20b", "model_name": "openai/gpt-oss-20b", "prompt_condition": "role_rubric", "decoding": "soft", "thinking": true, "seed": 42, "claim_id": 56, "claim": "A Monoamine Oxidase candy bar would cheer up a depressed friend.", "stance": "attack", "argument_id": "rank_3", "argument": "If a MAO candy bar really improved depression, psychiatrists would just hand out candy instead of writing prescriptions or running therapy sessions.", "reference_rank": 3, "raw_output": "analysisWe need to evaluate the argument: \"If a MAO candy bar really improved depression, psychiatrists would just hand out candy instead of writing prescriptions or running therapy sessions.\" This is an attacking argument: to refute the claim that a Monoamine Oxidase candy bar would cheer up a depressed friend. The argument's logic: If it truly worked, psychiatrists would use it instead of standard treatments. But psychiatrists don't, so it's not effective. We need to consider factuality, relevance, logical quality, effectiveness.\n\nFactuality: Does the statement about psychiatrists handing out candy instead of prescriptions/therapy if it worked hold? It's a hypothetical; it's not a factual claim about current practice. It's an inference. But is it plausible? If a candy bar were effective, psychiatrists might adopt it. But we don't know if they'd. The statement assumes psychiatrists would adopt it if effective. That may be overly simplistic. Also, psychiatrists might not hand out candy even if it worked because of regulations, evidence, etc. So factuality is uncertain.\n\nRelevance: It addresses the claim directly by challenging the claim that the candy would cheer up a depressed friend. It attempts to show that if it worked, psychiatrists would use it. So relevant.\n\nLogical quality: The argument is a form of argument from ignorance or argument from silence: \"If it worked, psychiatrists would use it, but they don't, so it doesn't work.\" This is a weak inference: absence of evidence is not evidence of absence. Also, psychiatrists might not adopt it for other reasons (cost, regulations, side effects). So the reasoning is weak.\n\nEffectiveness: It's not very strong. It might be somewhat persuasive but not compelling.\n\nThus overall score maybe around 30? Let's think: The argument is weak due to fallacious reasoning. It also makes an assumption that psychiatrists would adopt it if it worked, which might not be true. So it's not strong. Score maybe 25.\n\nI'll choose 30 to be safe. Score: 30.assistantfinalScore: 30", "score": null, "score_parse_ok": false, "score_parse_error": "no_labelled_score", "format_ok": false, "format_error": "expected_exact_score_format", "generated_tokens": 427, "hit_max_new_tokens": false, "generation_seconds": 27.66123696602881, "metadata": {"effective_generation_seed": 1674243664, "max_new_tokens_setting": "context_window", "max_new_tokens_limit": 130752}}
